      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIM",
          "content": "To investigate the symptom presentation and quality of life in obese Chinese patients with gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Data from patients diagnosed with GERD according to the Montreal definition, were collected between January 2009 to March 2010. The enrolled patients were assigned to the normal [body mass index (BMI) < 25 kg/m(2)], overweight (25-30 kg/m(2)), and obese (BMI > 30 kg/m(2)) groups. General demographic data, endoscopic findings, and quality of life of the three groups of patients were analyzed and compared."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Among the 173 enrolled patients, 102, 56 and 15 patients were classified in the normal, overweight, and obese, respectively. There was significantly more erosive esophagitis (73.3% vs 64.3% vs 39.2%, P = 0.002), hiatal hernia (60% vs 33.9% vs 16.7%, P = 0.001), and males (73.3% vs 73.2% vs 32.4%, P = 0.001) in the obese cases. The severity and frequency of heartburn, not acid regurgitation, was positively correlated with BMI, with a significant association in men, but not in women. Obese patients were prone to have low quality of life scores, with obese women having the lowest scores for mental health."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "In patients with GERD, obese men had the most severe endoscopic and clinical presentation. Obese women had the poorest mental health."
        }
      ]},
